{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PWjgp2fY2tmZk1Dzx635kv","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.LeaseStorage do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  Persistent lease storage using Mnesia.\n     4→\n     5→  Provides a durable, transactional storage layer for DHCP leases with\n     6→  support for multiple lease states and comprehensive querying.\n     7→\n     8→  ## Lease States\n     9→\n    10→  - `:offered` - IP address offered to client, awaiting REQUEST\n    11→  - `:active` - Lease active and in use by client\n    12→  - `:released` - Client released the lease voluntarily\n    13→  - `:expired` - Lease exceeded its lifetime\n    14→  - `:declined` - Client declined the offered IP (conflict detected)\n    15→\n    16→  ## Storage Modes\n    17→\n    18→  - `:disc_copies` - Persistent storage (default for production)\n    19→  - `:ram_copies` - Memory-only storage (for testing)\n    20→  \"\"\"\n    21→\n    22→  require Record\n    23→\n    24→  alias YellowDog.Dhcpv4.AddressPool\n    25→\n    26→  @type ip_address :: AddressPool.ip_address()\n    27→  @type mac_address :: binary()\n    28→  @type client_id :: binary() | nil\n    29→  @type lease_state :: :offered | :active | :released | :expired | :declined\n    30→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017aewAbAZ8fZWMahUzBSjcc","session_id":"0d3ee750-7f11-4e1e-a617-3b37d644afe9","uuid":"93ff806a-bd1e-4d6e-a66a-577850386021","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/yellow_dog_dhcpv4/lib/yellow_dog/dhcpv4/lease_storage.ex","content":"defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.LeaseStorage do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  Persistent lease storage using Mnesia.\n\n  Provides a durable, transactional storage layer for DHCP leases with\n  support for multiple lease states and comprehensive querying.\n\n  ## Lease States\n\n  - `:offered` - IP address offered to client, awaiting REQUEST\n  - `:active` - Lease active and in use by client\n  - `:released` - Client released the lease voluntarily\n  - `:expired` - Lease exceeded its lifetime\n  - `:declined` - Client declined the offered IP (conflict detected)\n\n  ## Storage Modes\n\n  - `:disc_copies` - Persistent storage (default for production)\n  - `:ram_copies` - Memory-only storage (for testing)\n  \"\"\"\n\n  require Record\n\n  alias YellowDog.Dhcpv4.AddressPool\n\n  @type ip_address :: AddressPool.ip_address()\n  @type mac_address :: binary()\n  @type client_id :: binary() | nil\n  @type lease_state :: :offered | :active | :released | :expired | :declined\n","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":639}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019KxLgkJuqWmPeQ4jD8paoR","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.PoolStats do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  Per-pool statistics and utilization tracking for DHCPv4.\n     4→\n     5→  Provides detailed metrics for each address pool including:\n     6→  - Total capacity and available addresses\n     7→  - Utilization percentage\n     8→  - Lease distribution by state\n     9→  - Allocation rates and trends\n    10→  \"\"\"\n    11→\n    12→  alias YellowDog.Dhcpv4.{AddressPool, LeaseStorage}\n    13→\n    14→  @type pool_stats :: %{\n    15→          pool_name: String.t(),\n    16→          total_addresses: non_neg_integer(),\n    17→          allocated_addresses: non_neg_integer(),\n    18→          available_addresses: non_neg_integer(),\n    19→          utilization_percent: float(),\n    20→          static_reservations: non_neg_integer(),\n    21→          leases_by_state: %{atom() => non_neg_integer()},\n    22→          ranges: [AddressPool.ip_range()],\n    23→          excluded_ranges: [AddressPool.ip_range()]\n    24→        }\n    25→\n    26→  @doc \"\"\"\n    27→  Gets comprehensive statistics for a specific pool.\n    28→\n    29→  ## Parameters\n    30→  - `pool` - Pool configuration\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017aewAbAZ8fZWMahUzBSjcc","session_id":"0d3ee750-7f11-4e1e-a617-3b37d644afe9","uuid":"30ee88ea-3bd0-41ae-bf44-abbb8a2aab57","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/yellow_dog_dhcpv4/lib/yellow_dog/dhcpv4/pool_stats.ex","content":"defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.PoolStats do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  Per-pool statistics and utilization tracking for DHCPv4.\n\n  Provides detailed metrics for each address pool including:\n  - Total capacity and available addresses\n  - Utilization percentage\n  - Lease distribution by state\n  - Allocation rates and trends\n  \"\"\"\n\n  alias YellowDog.Dhcpv4.{AddressPool, LeaseStorage}\n\n  @type pool_stats :: %{\n          pool_name: String.t(),\n          total_addresses: non_neg_integer(),\n          allocated_addresses: non_neg_integer(),\n          available_addresses: non_neg_integer(),\n          utilization_percent: float(),\n          static_reservations: non_neg_integer(),\n          leases_by_state: %{atom() => non_neg_integer()},\n          ranges: [AddressPool.ip_range()],\n          excluded_ranges: [AddressPool.ip_range()]\n        }\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Gets comprehensive statistics for a specific pool.\n\n  ## Parameters\n  - `pool` - Pool configuration","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":286}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D1AQagKAHDemB2j24gxGMA","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.ConfigWatcher do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  Watches for changes to the DHCPv4 configuration file and triggers reloads.\n     4→\n     5→  Uses FileSystem to monitor configuration files for changes and automatically\n     6→  reload pool configurations without requiring a server restart.\n     7→\n     8→  ## Features\n     9→  - Monitors configuration file for changes\n    10→  - Debounces rapid file changes\n    11→  - Validates configuration before applying\n    12→  - Emits telemetry events for reload operations\n    13→  - Graceful error handling\n    14→\n    15→  ## Example\n    16→      # Start the watcher\n    17→      {:ok, pid} = YellowDog.Dhcpv4.ConfigWatcher.start_link(\n    18→        config_file: \"/etc/yellowdog/dhcpv4.toml\",\n    19→        reload_callback: &MyModule.reload_config/1\n    20→      )\n    21→\n    22→      # The watcher will automatically reload configuration when the file changes\n    23→  \"\"\"\n    24→\n    25→  use GenServer\n    26→\n    27→  @type config_callback :: (map() -> :ok | {:error, term()})\n    28→\n    29→  @debounce_ms 1000\n    30→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017aewAbAZ8fZWMahUzBSjcc","session_id":"0d3ee750-7f11-4e1e-a617-3b37d644afe9","uuid":"64076226-b6a1-4941-bd4d-03e20af95683","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/yellow_dog_dhcpv4/lib/yellow_dog/dhcpv4/config_watcher.ex","content":"defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.ConfigWatcher do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  Watches for changes to the DHCPv4 configuration file and triggers reloads.\n\n  Uses FileSystem to monitor configuration files for changes and automatically\n  reload pool configurations without requiring a server restart.\n\n  ## Features\n  - Monitors configuration file for changes\n  - Debounces rapid file changes\n  - Validates configuration before applying\n  - Emits telemetry events for reload operations\n  - Graceful error handling\n\n  ## Example\n      # Start the watcher\n      {:ok, pid} = YellowDog.Dhcpv4.ConfigWatcher.start_link(\n        config_file: \"/etc/yellowdog/dhcpv4.toml\",\n        reload_callback: &MyModule.reload_config/1\n      )\n\n      # The watcher will automatically reload configuration when the file changes\n  \"\"\"\n\n  use GenServer\n\n  @type config_callback :: (map() -> :ok | {:error, term()})\n\n  @debounce_ms 1000\n","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":348}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XbVCobT97XtCZoGrpcbZjn","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv6.RelayAgent do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  DHCPv6 Relay Agent support for multi-hop message forwarding.\n     4→\n     5→  Handles RELAY-FORWARD and RELAY-REPLY messages, allowing the DHCPv6 server\n     6→  to communicate with clients on different network segments through relay agents.\n     7→\n     8→  ## Relay Agent Flow\n     9→\n    10→  ```\n    11→  Client ──▶ Relay Agent ──▶ DHCPv6 Server\n    12→               (RELAY-FORW)\n    13→\n    14→  Client ◀── Relay Agent ◀── DHCPv6 Server\n    15→               (RELAY-REPL)\n    16→  ```\n    17→\n    18→  ## Relay Message Format\n    19→\n    20→  RELAY-FORWARD:\n    21→  - msg_type (1 byte): 12\n    22→  - hop_count (1 byte): Number of relay agents traversed\n    23→  - link_address (16 bytes): Address on the link where the client is located\n    24→  - peer_address (16 bytes): Address of the client or previous relay agent\n    25→  - options: Encapsulated client message and relay agent options\n    26→\n    27→  RELAY-REPLY:\n    28→  - msg_type (1 byte): 13\n    29→  - hop_count (1 byte): Must match RELAY-FORWARD\n    30→  - link_address (16 bytes): Copied from RELAY-FORWARD\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017aewAbAZ8fZWMahUzBSjcc","session_id":"0d3ee750-7f11-4e1e-a617-3b37d644afe9","uuid":"56c7431d-c856-40c9-864f-e327733fcae8","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/yellow_dog_dhcpv6/lib/yellow_dog/dhcpv6/relay_agent.ex","content":"defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv6.RelayAgent do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  DHCPv6 Relay Agent support for multi-hop message forwarding.\n\n  Handles RELAY-FORWARD and RELAY-REPLY messages, allowing the DHCPv6 server\n  to communicate with clients on different network segments through relay agents.\n\n  ## Relay Agent Flow\n\n  ```\n  Client ──▶ Relay Agent ──▶ DHCPv6 Server\n               (RELAY-FORW)\n\n  Client ◀── Relay Agent ◀── DHCPv6 Server\n               (RELAY-REPL)\n  ```\n\n  ## Relay Message Format\n\n  RELAY-FORWARD:\n  - msg_type (1 byte): 12\n  - hop_count (1 byte): Number of relay agents traversed\n  - link_address (16 bytes): Address on the link where the client is located\n  - peer_address (16 bytes): Address of the client or previous relay agent\n  - options: Encapsulated client message and relay agent options\n\n  RELAY-REPLY:\n  - msg_type (1 byte): 13\n  - hop_count (1 byte): Must match RELAY-FORWARD\n  - link_address (16 bytes): Copied from RELAY-FORWARD","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":254}}}
