{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011WCrypx9pPULQDkJwQboCf","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.AddressPool do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  IP address pool management for DHCPv4.\n     4→\n     5→  Manages IP address ranges, allocation, and availability tracking.\n     6→  Supports multiple pools, static reservations, and address conflict detection.\n     7→  \"\"\"\n     8→\n     9→  @type ip_address :: {0..255, 0..255, 0..255, 0..255}\n    10→  @type mac_address :: binary()\n    11→  @type ip_range :: {ip_address(), ip_address()}\n    12→  @type pool_config :: %{\n    13→          name: String.t(),\n    14→          ranges: [ip_range()],\n    15→          # Legacy support - converted to ranges\n    16→          range_start: ip_address() | nil,\n    17→          range_end: ip_address() | nil,\n    18→          excluded_ranges: [ip_range()],\n    19→          subnet_mask: ip_address(),\n    20→          gateway: ip_address(),\n    21→          dns_servers: [ip_address()],\n    22→          domain_name: String.t() | nil,\n    23→          lease_time: pos_integer()\n    24→        }\n    25→\n    26→  @doc \"\"\"\n    27→  Creates a new address pool from configuration.\n    28→\n    29→  ## Parameters\n    30→  - `config` - Pool configuration map\n    31→\n    32→  ## Returns\n    33→  - `{:ok, pool}` - Successfully created pool\n    34→  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to create pool\n    35→\n    36→  ## Example\n    37→      iex> config = %{\n    38→      ...>   name: \"main\",\n    39→      ...>   range_start: {192, 168, 1, 100},\n    40→      ...>   range_end: {192, 168, 1, 200},\n    41→      ...>   subnet_mask: {255, 255, 255, 0},\n    42→      ...>   gateway: {192, 168, 1, 1},\n    43→      ...>   dns_servers: [{192, 168, 1, 1}],\n    44→      ...>   domain_name: \"local\",\n    45→      ...>   lease_time: 86400\n    46→      ...> }\n    47→      iex> YellowDog.Dhcpv4.AddressPool.new(config)\n    48→      {:ok, %{...}}\n    49→  \"\"\"\n    50→  @spec new(map()) :: {:ok, pool_config()} | {:error, term()}\n    51→  def new(config) do\n    52→    with {:ok, validated_config} <- validate_pool_config(config) do\n    53→      # Support both legacy (range_start/range_end) and new (ranges) format\n    54→      ranges = build_ranges(config, validated_config)\n    55→      excluded_ranges = build_excluded_ranges(config)\n    56→\n    57→      # Get first range for defaults\n    58→      {first_range_start, _} = hd(ranges)\n    59→\n    60→      pool = %{\n    61→        name: Map.get(config, :name, \"default\"),\n    62→        ranges: ranges,\n    63→        range_start: validated_config[:range_start],\n    64→        range_end: validated_config[:range_end],\n    65→        excluded_ranges: excluded_ranges,\n    66→        subnet_mask: Map.get(config, :subnet_mask, {255, 255, 255, 0}),\n    67→        gateway: Map.get(config, :gateway, first_range_start),\n    68→        dns_servers: Map.get(config, :dns_servers, [first_range_start]),\n    69→        domain_name: Map.get(config, :domain_name),\n    70→        lease_time: Map.get(config, :lease_time, 86400),\n    71→        static_reservations: Map.get(config, :static_reservations, %{})\n    72→      }\n    73→\n    74→      {:ok, pool}\n    75→    end\n    76→  end\n    77→\n    78→  @doc \"\"\"\n    79→  Validates pool configuration.\n    80→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JV4Wn46h4DTx7pTeGA3eHu","session_id":"b20a2ce5-4d1a-4dfb-8234-d009f9779d17","uuid":"def8860f-7f9b-4781-8bd4-8c60bfd3eda6","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/yellow_dog_dhcpv4/lib/yellow_dog/dhcpv4/address_pool.ex","content":"defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.AddressPool do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  IP address pool management for DHCPv4.\n\n  Manages IP address ranges, allocation, and availability tracking.\n  Supports multiple pools, static reservations, and address conflict detection.\n  \"\"\"\n\n  @type ip_address :: {0..255, 0..255, 0..255, 0..255}\n  @type mac_address :: binary()\n  @type ip_range :: {ip_address(), ip_address()}\n  @type pool_config :: %{\n          name: String.t(),\n          ranges: [ip_range()],\n          # Legacy support - converted to ranges\n          range_start: ip_address() | nil,\n          range_end: ip_address() | nil,\n          excluded_ranges: [ip_range()],\n          subnet_mask: ip_address(),\n          gateway: ip_address(),\n          dns_servers: [ip_address()],\n          domain_name: String.t() | nil,\n          lease_time: pos_integer()\n        }\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Creates a new address pool from configuration.\n\n  ## Parameters\n  - `config` - Pool configuration map\n\n  ## Returns\n  - `{:ok, pool}` - Successfully created pool\n  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to create pool\n\n  ## Example\n      iex> config = %{\n      ...>   name: \"main\",\n      ...>   range_start: {192, 168, 1, 100},\n      ...>   range_end: {192, 168, 1, 200},\n      ...>   subnet_mask: {255, 255, 255, 0},\n      ...>   gateway: {192, 168, 1, 1},\n      ...>   dns_servers: [{192, 168, 1, 1}],\n      ...>   domain_name: \"local\",\n      ...>   lease_time: 86400\n      ...> }\n      iex> YellowDog.Dhcpv4.AddressPool.new(config)\n      {:ok, %{...}}\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec new(map()) :: {:ok, pool_config()} | {:error, term()}\n  def new(config) do\n    with {:ok, validated_config} <- validate_pool_config(config) do\n      # Support both legacy (range_start/range_end) and new (ranges) format\n      ranges = build_ranges(config, validated_config)\n      excluded_ranges = build_excluded_ranges(config)\n\n      # Get first range for defaults\n      {first_range_start, _} = hd(ranges)\n\n      pool = %{\n        name: Map.get(config, :name, \"default\"),\n        ranges: ranges,\n        range_start: validated_config[:range_start],\n        range_end: validated_config[:range_end],\n        excluded_ranges: excluded_ranges,\n        subnet_mask: Map.get(config, :subnet_mask, {255, 255, 255, 0}),\n        gateway: Map.get(config, :gateway, first_range_start),\n        dns_servers: Map.get(config, :dns_servers, [first_range_start]),\n        domain_name: Map.get(config, :domain_name),\n        lease_time: Map.get(config, :lease_time, 86400),\n        static_reservations: Map.get(config, :static_reservations, %{})\n      }\n\n      {:ok, pool}\n    end\n  end\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Validates pool configuration.\n","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":412}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GyhXecLTTmBNUtEFjfxRSw","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.ConfigWatcher do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  Watches for changes to the DHCPv4 configuration file and triggers reloads.\n     4→\n     5→  Uses FileSystem to monitor configuration files for changes and automatically\n     6→  reload pool configurations without requiring a server restart.\n     7→\n     8→  ## Features\n     9→  - Monitors configuration file for changes\n    10→  - Debounces rapid file changes\n    11→  - Validates configuration before applying\n    12→  - Emits telemetry events for reload operations\n    13→  - Graceful error handling\n    14→\n    15→  ## Example\n    16→      # Start the watcher\n    17→      {:ok, pid} = YellowDog.Dhcpv4.ConfigWatcher.start_link(\n    18→        config_file: \"/etc/yellowdog/dhcpv4.toml\",\n    19→        reload_callback: &MyModule.reload_config/1\n    20→      )\n    21→\n    22→      # The watcher will automatically reload configuration when the file changes\n    23→  \"\"\"\n    24→\n    25→  use GenServer\n    26→\n    27→  @type config_callback :: (map() -> :ok | {:error, term()})\n    28→\n    29→  @debounce_ms 1000\n    30→\n    31→  ## Client API\n    32→\n    33→  @doc \"\"\"\n    34→  Starts the ConfigWatcher GenServer.\n    35→\n    36→  ## Options\n    37→  - `:config_file` - Path to configuration file to watch (required)\n    38→  - `:reload_callback` - Function to call when config changes (required)\n    39→  - `:enabled` - Whether to enable watching (default: true)\n    40→  - `:debounce_ms` - Milliseconds to wait before reloading (default: 1000)\n    41→\n    42→  ## Returns\n    43→  - `{:ok, pid}` - Successfully started\n    44→  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to start\n    45→  \"\"\"\n    46→  @spec start_link(keyword()) :: GenServer.on_start()\n    47→  def start_link(opts \\\\ []) do\n    48→    GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, opts, name: __MODULE__)\n    49→  end\n    50→\n    51→  @doc \"\"\"\n    52→  Manually triggers a configuration reload.\n    53→\n    54→  ## Returns\n    55→  - `:ok` - Reload successful\n    56→  - `{:error, reason}` - Reload failed\n    57→  \"\"\"\n    58→  @spec reload() :: :ok | {:error, term()}\n    59→  def reload do\n    60→    GenServer.call(__MODULE__, :reload)\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JV4Wn46h4DTx7pTeGA3eHu","session_id":"b20a2ce5-4d1a-4dfb-8234-d009f9779d17","uuid":"4a8b22f1-10f3-48f0-bd59-fcf959c176e7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/yellow_dog_dhcpv4/lib/yellow_dog/dhcpv4/config_watcher.ex","content":"defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.ConfigWatcher do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  Watches for changes to the DHCPv4 configuration file and triggers reloads.\n\n  Uses FileSystem to monitor configuration files for changes and automatically\n  reload pool configurations without requiring a server restart.\n\n  ## Features\n  - Monitors configuration file for changes\n  - Debounces rapid file changes\n  - Validates configuration before applying\n  - Emits telemetry events for reload operations\n  - Graceful error handling\n\n  ## Example\n      # Start the watcher\n      {:ok, pid} = YellowDog.Dhcpv4.ConfigWatcher.start_link(\n        config_file: \"/etc/yellowdog/dhcpv4.toml\",\n        reload_callback: &MyModule.reload_config/1\n      )\n\n      # The watcher will automatically reload configuration when the file changes\n  \"\"\"\n\n  use GenServer\n\n  @type config_callback :: (map() -> :ok | {:error, term()})\n\n  @debounce_ms 1000\n\n  ## Client API\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Starts the ConfigWatcher GenServer.\n\n  ## Options\n  - `:config_file` - Path to configuration file to watch (required)\n  - `:reload_callback` - Function to call when config changes (required)\n  - `:enabled` - Whether to enable watching (default: true)\n  - `:debounce_ms` - Milliseconds to wait before reloading (default: 1000)\n\n  ## Returns\n  - `{:ok, pid}` - Successfully started\n  - `{:error, reason}` - Failed to start\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec start_link(keyword()) :: GenServer.on_start()\n  def start_link(opts \\\\ []) do\n    GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, opts, name: __MODULE__)\n  end\n\n  @doc \"\"\"\n  Manually triggers a configuration reload.\n\n  ## Returns\n  - `:ok` - Reload successful\n  - `{:error, reason}` - Reload failed\n  \"\"\"\n  @spec reload() :: :ok | {:error, term()}\n  def reload do\n    GenServer.call(__MODULE__, :reload)","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":348}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P4Z2FifxhsjrA29h2YFRwr","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.LeaseStorage do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  Persistent lease storage using Mnesia.\n     4→\n     5→  Provides a durable, transactional storage layer for DHCP leases with\n     6→  support for multiple lease states and comprehensive querying.\n     7→\n     8→  ## Lease States\n     9→\n    10→  - `:offered` - IP address offered to client, awaiting REQUEST\n    11→  - `:active` - Lease active and in use by client\n    12→  - `:released` - Client released the lease voluntarily\n    13→  - `:expired` - Lease exceeded its lifetime\n    14→  - `:declined` - Client declined the offered IP (conflict detected)\n    15→\n    16→  ## Storage Modes\n    17→\n    18→  - `:disc_copies` - Persistent storage (default for production)\n    19→  - `:ram_copies` - Memory-only storage (for testing)\n    20→  \"\"\"\n    21→\n    22→  require Record\n    23→\n    24→  alias YellowDog.Dhcpv4.AddressPool\n    25→\n    26→  @type ip_address :: AddressPool.ip_address()\n    27→  @type mac_address :: binary()\n    28→  @type client_id :: binary() | nil\n    29→  @type lease_state :: :offered | :active | :released | :expired | :declined\n    30→\n    31→  @type lease :: %{\n    32→          mac_address: mac_address(),\n    33→          ip_address: ip_address(),\n    34→          pool_name: String.t(),\n    35→          state: lease_state(),\n    36→          lease_time: pos_integer(),\n    37→          expires_at: integer(),\n    38→          hostname: String.t() | nil,\n    39→          client_id: client_id(),\n    40→          created_at: integer(),\n    41→          updated_at: integer()\n    42→        }\n    43→\n    44→  # Define Mnesia record\n    45→  Record.defrecord(:lease_record,\n    46→    mac_address: nil,\n    47→    ip_address: nil,\n    48→    pool_name: nil,\n    49→    state: :offered,\n    50→    lease_time: 0,\n    51→    expires_at: 0,\n    52→    hostname: nil,\n    53→    client_id: nil,\n    54→    created_at: 0,\n    55→    updated_at: 0\n    56→  )\n    57→\n    58→  @table_name :dhcpv4_leases\n    59→\n    60→  ## Public API\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JV4Wn46h4DTx7pTeGA3eHu","session_id":"b20a2ce5-4d1a-4dfb-8234-d009f9779d17","uuid":"599d4dc9-ee58-48ad-9b6c-ce55829df2c2","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/yellow_dog_dhcpv4/lib/yellow_dog/dhcpv4/lease_storage.ex","content":"defmodule YellowDog.Dhcpv4.LeaseStorage do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  Persistent lease storage using Mnesia.\n\n  Provides a durable, transactional storage layer for DHCP leases with\n  support for multiple lease states and comprehensive querying.\n\n  ## Lease States\n\n  - `:offered` - IP address offered to client, awaiting REQUEST\n  - `:active` - Lease active and in use by client\n  - `:released` - Client released the lease voluntarily\n  - `:expired` - Lease exceeded its lifetime\n  - `:declined` - Client declined the offered IP (conflict detected)\n\n  ## Storage Modes\n\n  - `:disc_copies` - Persistent storage (default for production)\n  - `:ram_copies` - Memory-only storage (for testing)\n  \"\"\"\n\n  require Record\n\n  alias YellowDog.Dhcpv4.AddressPool\n\n  @type ip_address :: AddressPool.ip_address()\n  @type mac_address :: binary()\n  @type client_id :: binary() | nil\n  @type lease_state :: :offered | :active | :released | :expired | :declined\n\n  @type lease :: %{\n          mac_address: mac_address(),\n          ip_address: ip_address(),\n          pool_name: String.t(),\n          state: lease_state(),\n          lease_time: pos_integer(),\n          expires_at: integer(),\n          hostname: String.t() | nil,\n          client_id: client_id(),\n          created_at: integer(),\n          updated_at: integer()\n        }\n\n  # Define Mnesia record\n  Record.defrecord(:lease_record,\n    mac_address: nil,\n    ip_address: nil,\n    pool_name: nil,\n    state: :offered,\n    lease_time: 0,\n    expires_at: 0,\n    hostname: nil,\n    client_id: nil,\n    created_at: 0,\n    updated_at: 0\n  )\n\n  @table_name :dhcpv4_leases\n\n  ## Public API","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":639}}}
