{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QwRqYZtuGX9xJePDL28rQt","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule DNS.Message do\n     2→  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n     3→  # DNS Message\n     4→\n     5→  All communications inside of the domain protocol are carried in a single\n     6→  format called a message.  The top level format of message is divided\n     7→  into 5 sections (some of which are empty in certain cases) shown below:\n     8→\n     9→      +---------------------+\n    10→      |        Header       |\n    11→      +---------------------+\n    12→      |       Question      | the question for the name server\n    13→      +---------------------+\n    14→      |        Answer       | RRs answering the question\n    15→      +---------------------+\n    16→      |      Authority      | RRs pointing toward an authority\n    17→      +---------------------+\n    18→      |      Additional     | RRs holding additional information\n    19→      +---------------------+\n    20→\n    21→  The header section is always present.  The header includes fields that\n    22→  specify which of the remaining sections are present, and also specify\n    23→  whether the message is a query or a response, a standard query or some\n    24→  other opcode, etc.\n    25→\n    26→  The names of the sections after the header are derived from their use in\n    27→  standard queries.  The question section contains fields that describe a\n    28→  question to a name server.  These fields are a query type (QTYPE), a\n    29→  query class (QCLASS), and a query domain name (QNAME).  The last three\n    30→  sections have the same format: a possibly empty list of concatenated\n    31→  resource records (RRs).  The answer section contains RRs that answer the\n    32→  question; the authority section contains RRs that point toward an\n    33→  authoritative name server; the additional records section contains RRs\n    34→  which relate to the query, but are not strictly answers for the\n    35→  question.\n    36→\n    37→  \"\"\"\n    38→\n    39→  # alias DNS.Message\n    40→  alias DNS.Message.Header\n    41→  alias DNS.Message.Question\n    42→  alias DNS.Message.Record\n    43→  alias DNS.ResourceRecordType, as: RRType\n    44→\n    45→  @type t :: %__MODULE__{\n    46→          header: %Header{},\n    47→          # Question list\n    48→          qdlist: list(%Question{}),\n    49→          # Answer list\n    50→          anlist: list(%Record{}),\n    51→          # Authority list\n    52→          nslist: list(%Record{}),\n    53→          # Additional record list\n    54→          arlist: list(%Record{}),\n    55→          options: Keyword.t()\n    56→        }\n    57→\n    58→  defstruct header: %Header{},\n    59→            qdlist: [],\n    60→            anlist: [],\n    61→            nslist: [],\n    62→            arlist: [],\n    63→            options: []\n    64→\n    65→  def new() do\n    66→    %__MODULE__{\n    67→      header: Header.new(),\n    68→      qdlist: [],\n    69→      anlist: [],\n    70→      nslist: [],\n    71→      arlist: [],\n    72→      options: []\n    73→    }\n    74→  end\n    75→\n    76→  def from_iodata(<<header_bytes::binary-size(12), _::binary>> = message) do\n    77→    header = Header.from_iodata(header_bytes)\n    78→\n    79→    {qdlist, qd_size} = Question.list_from_message(message, header.qdcount)\n    80→\n    81→    {anlist, offset} = Record.list_from_message(header.ancount, message, 12 + qd_size)\n    82→\n    83→    {nslist, offset} =\n    84→      Record.list_from_message(header.nscount, message, offset)\n    85→\n    86→    {arlist, _} =\n    87→      Record.list_from_message(header.arcount, message, offset)\n    88→\n    89→    %__MODULE__{\n    90→      header: header,\n    91→      qdlist: qdlist,\n    92→      anlist: anlist,\n    93→      nslist: nslist,\n    94→      arlist: arlist\n    95→    }\n    96→  end\n    97→\n    98→  def update_header_attr(%__MODULE__{header: header} = message, key, value) do\n    99→    header = Map.put(header, key, value)\n   100→    %{message | header: header}\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84996b7b-e98e-4515-80e7-3bf6c146a135","uuid":"3361f4e1-2286-44c1-a939-dd5c3d7d7af6","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/gao/Workspace/gsmlg-dev/yellow-dog/apps/ex_dns/lib/dns/message.ex","content":"defmodule DNS.Message do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  # DNS Message\n\n  All communications inside of the domain protocol are carried in a single\n  format called a message.  The top level format of message is divided\n  into 5 sections (some of which are empty in certain cases) shown below:\n\n      +---------------------+\n      |        Header       |\n      +---------------------+\n      |       Question      | the question for the name server\n      +---------------------+\n      |        Answer       | RRs answering the question\n      +---------------------+\n      |      Authority      | RRs pointing toward an authority\n      +---------------------+\n      |      Additional     | RRs holding additional information\n      +---------------------+\n\n  The header section is always present.  The header includes fields that\n  specify which of the remaining sections are present, and also specify\n  whether the message is a query or a response, a standard query or some\n  other opcode, etc.\n\n  The names of the sections after the header are derived from their use in\n  standard queries.  The question section contains fields that describe a\n  question to a name server.  These fields are a query type (QTYPE), a\n  query class (QCLASS), and a query domain name (QNAME).  The last three\n  sections have the same format: a possibly empty list of concatenated\n  resource records (RRs).  The answer section contains RRs that answer the\n  question; the authority section contains RRs that point toward an\n  authoritative name server; the additional records section contains RRs\n  which relate to the query, but are not strictly answers for the\n  question.\n\n  \"\"\"\n\n  # alias DNS.Message\n  alias DNS.Message.Header\n  alias DNS.Message.Question\n  alias DNS.Message.Record\n  alias DNS.ResourceRecordType, as: RRType\n\n  @type t :: %__MODULE__{\n          header: %Header{},\n          # Question list\n          qdlist: list(%Question{}),\n          # Answer list\n          anlist: list(%Record{}),\n          # Authority list\n          nslist: list(%Record{}),\n          # Additional record list\n          arlist: list(%Record{}),\n          options: Keyword.t()\n        }\n\n  defstruct header: %Header{},\n            qdlist: [],\n            anlist: [],\n            nslist: [],\n            arlist: [],\n            options: []\n\n  def new() do\n    %__MODULE__{\n      header: Header.new(),\n      qdlist: [],\n      anlist: [],\n      nslist: [],\n      arlist: [],\n      options: []\n    }\n  end\n\n  def from_iodata(<<header_bytes::binary-size(12), _::binary>> = message) do\n    header = Header.from_iodata(header_bytes)\n\n    {qdlist, qd_size} = Question.list_from_message(message, header.qdcount)\n\n    {anlist, offset} = Record.list_from_message(header.ancount, message, 12 + qd_size)\n\n    {nslist, offset} =\n      Record.list_from_message(header.nscount, message, offset)\n\n    {arlist, _} =\n      Record.list_from_message(header.arcount, message, offset)\n\n    %__MODULE__{\n      header: header,\n      qdlist: qdlist,\n      anlist: anlist,\n      nslist: nslist,\n      arlist: arlist\n    }\n  end\n\n  def update_header_attr(%__MODULE__{header: header} = message, key, value) do\n    header = Map.put(header, key, value)\n    %{message | header: header}","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":166}}}
